# SYNOPSIS

```usage
sed [-nrE] script [file ...]
sed [-nrE] -e script [-e script] ... [-f scriptfile] ... [file ...]
sed [-nrE] [-e script] ... -f scriptfile [-f scriptfile] ... [file ...]
```

# DESCRIPTION

`sed` reads each <file> in order, or standard input when there is none or for a <file> of `-`, applies a script of editing commands to each line, and writes the result on standard output.
The script is the first argument, or, when there is a `-e` or `-f`, each `-e` <script> and each `-f` <scriptfile>'s lines, in the order given.

: `-n`
  Write a line only when the script says to.
  A script whose first line begins `#n` does the same.
: `-E`, `-r`
  Read regular expressions as POSIX's extended ones instead of its basic ones.

For each line of input, `sed` puts the line, without its newline, in the pattern space, runs the commands that are addressed to it in order, then writes the pattern space and a newline unless `-n` was given.
A second buffer, the hold space, keeps text from one line to the next.
A <file> that cannot be opened is reported and the next one read.

## Addresses

A command may have no address, and is then run for every line; one, and is run for the lines it selects; or two separated by `,`, and is run for each range of lines from one that the first selects to the next that the second does, inclusive.
When the second is a line number at or before the line that started the range, the range is that one line.
A `!` after the address runs the command for the lines not selected instead.

: <n>
  Line <n>, counted across all the files.
: `$`
  The last line of the last file.
: `/`<re>`/`, `\`<c><re><c>
  The lines that <re> matches; <c> is any character but backslash or newline, and within <re> `\`<c> is a <c>.
  An empty <re> is the last one used.

## Commands

Commands are separated by newlines or `;`.
Blanks before a command and after its address are ignored, and `#` begins a comment.
Each command below takes at most the number of addresses after it in parentheses.

: `{` (2)
  Run the commands up to the matching `}` for the lines selected.
: `a` <text> (1)
  Write <text> before the next line is read, or at the end.
: `b` <label> (2)
  Branch to the `:` <label>, or to the end of the script when there is none.
: `c` <text> (2)
  Delete the pattern space, and write <text>; with two addresses, at the end of the range.
: `d` (2)
  Delete the pattern space and start the next cycle.
: `D` (2)
  Delete the pattern space up to its first newline and start the next cycle without reading a new line; with no newline, as `d`.
: `g`, `G` (2)
  Replace the pattern space with the hold space, or add a newline and the hold space to it.
: `h`, `H` (2)
  Replace the hold space with the pattern space, or add a newline and the pattern space to it.
: `i` <text> (1)
  Write <text> now.
: `l` (2)
  Write the pattern space unambiguously: `\\`, `\a`, `\b`, `\f`, `\r`, `\t` and `\v` for those characters, other unprintable bytes in octal, and `$` at the end.
: `n` (2)
  Write the pattern space, unless `-n`, and replace it with the next line; at the end of input, quit.
: `N` (2)
  Add a newline and the next line to the pattern space; at the end of input, quit without writing the pattern space, as POSIX has it.
: `p`, `P` (2)
  Write the pattern space, or the pattern space up to its first newline.
: `q` (1)
  Write the pattern space, unless `-n`, and quit.
: `r` <file> (1)
  Write <file>'s contents before the next line is read; a <file> that cannot be read is empty.
: `s/`<re>`/`<replacement>`/`<flags> (2)
  Replace the first match of <re> in the pattern space with <replacement>.
  Any character but backslash and newline may stand for `/`.
  In <replacement>, `&` is the text matched, `\1` to `\9` the text the parenthesized subexpressions matched, `\n` a newline, and a backslash before a newline a newline.
  <flags> are any of: a number <n>, replace the <n>th match; `g`, replace every match; `p`, write the pattern space if a replacement was made; `w` <file>, append it to <file> if one was.
: `t` <label> (2)
  Branch to <label>, or to the end, if a replacement has been made since the last line was read or the last `t`.
: `w` <file> (2)
  Append the pattern space to <file>.
: `x` (2)
  Exchange the pattern and hold spaces.
: `y/`<set1>`/`<set2>`/` (2)
  Replace each character in <set1> with the one at the same place in <set2>, which must be as long.
: `:` <label> (0)
  The place a `b` or `t` to <label> goes.
: `=` (1)
  Write the line number and a newline.

<text> for `a`, `c` and `i` is the rest of the line; as POSIX writes it, the command is followed by `\` and a newline, and a line of the text ending in `\` is continued on the next.
A label or a <file> name ends at the end of the line, or, as sbase allows, at a `;` or `}`.
The files of `w` commands are created, or emptied, when the script is read, before any input is.

`sed` is sbase's, built into `sbase-box` with sbase's other commands and installed as `/boot/bin/posix/sed`, a hard link to it.
In the POSIX namespace (a manifest's `ns=posix`, made by `/lib/ns/posix`, see namespace(6)) `/boot/bin/posix` is bound before `/boot/bin` on `/bin`, so POSIX programs find it as `/bin/sed`.
There is no native `sed`.

# EXAMPLES

As `tests/posix/sbasetest.c` runs it in the POSIX namespace:

```text
% echo hello world | sed s/world/there/
hello there
```

